{"qid":"Q2086608","names":["Western Satraps"],"title":"Western Satraps","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Western Satraps (also called Kshatrapas) were a dynasty of Indo-Scythian (Saka) rulers who controlled the western Deccan, Gujarat, Malwa, and Rajasthan from roughly the 1st to early 5th century CE, founded by Chastana around 35 CE and descending from two main lineages — the Kshaharatas (ending c. 78 CE) and the longer-lived Kardamakas. Their most powerful ruler was Rudradaman I (r. c. 130–150 CE), whose Junagadh Rock inscription (150 CE) — the earliest lengthy Sanskrit prose inscription known — records his defeat of the Satavahanas twice and his repairs to the Sudarshana Lake dam in Kathiawar. The dynasty was finally extinguished around 415 CE when Chandragupta II of the Gupta Empire conquered Gujarat and absorbed their territory.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Territorial extent is attested by the Junagadh inscription, coin distributions (numerous silver and copper issues with ruler names and dates), and the Nasik cave records. Coin-based chronology is well established; the geographic frontier with Satavahanas and later Guptas is approximate, with moderate confidence.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Western Satraps","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_Satraps"},{"label":"Britannica: Satavahana dynasty","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Satavahana-dynasty"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Western Satraps","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Bhumaka","nativeName":"𑀪𑀽𑀫𑀓","roman":"Bhūmaka","start":35,"end":45,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":